Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.060 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  23 years ago today Hong Kong was  returned to China by the UK thanks to  the new national security law it has  never been under tighter control a  picture of discipline and order exactly  what the Chinese Communist Party wants  to ensure in Hong Kong with its sweeping  legislation Cheers  all that's called for celebration and so  Hong Kong's pro-beijing leader Carrie  Lam raised a toast it is an essential  and timely decision for restoring  stability in Hong Kong the significance  of this decision is multifaceted it  shows that the central government is  determined to restore stability in Hong  Kong after a year of escalating violence  and riots since last year but Hong  Kong's freedoms die hard they still came  out even though the cost of protest had  been raised significantly on the first  full day of the law this is a new police  flag for a new era a warning against  free speech and it didn't take long to  use this man was the first to be  arrested under the national security law  for carrying a flag that said Hong Kong  independence look closer though and the  flag says in a tiny font no to Hong Kong  independence it will be a test of the  new law and of authorities sense of  humor hitherto not renowned even though  nearly everything has changed some  things remain the same probably in the  future we may never see a million people  on the street again not because we  satisfied if the government but just  because we are now living in  but I still have hopes and do believe in  Hong Kong especially the younger  generation and the tactics were familiar  to less a police presence than an  omnipresence the water cannon promptly  deployed the so called Raptors snatch  squads at full pace and scores of  arrests the difference is that under the  new legislation the widely drawn crimes  of secession subversion terrorism and  colluding with foreign forces could  carry a life sentence last year the  numbers were bigger when there was no  national security law and no covert 19  restrictions on mass gatherings and the  March later turned violent with  protesters storming Hong Kong's  Parliament it is too soon to say how the  law will change all that much depends on  its application by police and its  interpretation by judges but if Beijing  meant to silence Hong Kong today the  people reminded the party they still  have a voice  Tom Cheshire Sky News Beijing

